---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import FlowDiagram from "../components/FlowDiagram.astro";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import { canonicalUrl } from "../utils/seo";

const SECTIONS = [
	{ id: "overview", label: "Architecture Overview" },
	{ id: "rendering", label: "Rendering Strategy" },
	{ id: "content", label: "Content Architecture" },
	{ id: "api", label: "API Architecture" },
	{ id: "ai", label: "AI Architecture" },
	{ id: "security", label: "Security" },
	{ id: "performance", label: "Performance" },
	{ id: "scalability", label: "Scalability" },
	{ id: "testing", label: "Testing" },
	{ id: "future", label: "Future Evolution" },
];
---

<Base
	title="EngiVault Architecture"
	description="How EngiVault is built on Astro and EmDash: rendering strategy, content architecture, the AI assistant's retrieval-augmented design, security, performance, and how the system could scale."
	canonical={canonicalUrl(Astro.url)}
>
	<div class="container page">
		<PageHeader
			eyebrow="Engineering Knowledge Intelligence"
			title="EngiVault Architecture"
			description="How the system is actually built — and, just as importantly, what was deliberately left out."
		/>

		<nav class="toc" aria-label="Page sections">
			{SECTIONS.map((s) => (
				<a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a>
			))}
		</nav>

		<section id="overview" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">1. Architecture Overview</h2>
			<p>
				EngiVault is a content site built <strong>on top of</strong> EmDash — an existing
				Astro-native CMS already present in this repository — not a new backend written
				from scratch. Every content type (articles, ADRs, incident reports) is a real,
				schema-defined EmDash collection with typed fields, not a hand-rolled data model.
				The only genuinely new server logic in the whole system is the Engineering AI
				Assistant's retrieval + LLM call.
			</p>
			<p>Two request flows cover essentially everything EngiVault does:</p>

			<h3 class="flow-title">Reading content</h3>
			<FlowDiagram steps={["Browser", "Astro (SSR)", "EmDash CMS / API", "Database"]} />
			<p class="flow-caption">
				Every page request — homepage, listings, a single article, search — runs this
				same path. Astro queries EmDash's Live Collections API on the server, EmDash
				resolves that against the database, and fully-rendered HTML goes back to the
				browser. No client-side data fetching, no separate backend.
			</p>

			<h3 class="flow-title">Asking the AI assistant</h3>
			<FlowDiagram steps={["Astro (/ask page)", "/api/ask", "Content Retrieval", "LLM"]} />
			<p class="flow-caption">
				The one exception to "just render content": a question posted from the{" "}
				<code>/ask</code> page hits this site's own <code>/api/ask</code> endpoint, which
				retrieves relevant EmDash content <em>before</em> calling the LLM, so the model
				answers from real documents instead of guessing.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="rendering" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">2. Rendering Strategy</h2>
			<p>
				Every page is server-rendered on request (Astro's <code>output: "server"</code>{" "}
				with the Node adapter) — there is no static-generation build step for content
				pages, and no client-side router.
			</p>
			<div class="why">
				<p class="why-label">Why server-first rendering</p>
				<p>
					EngiVault's content changes after deploy: an editor publishes an article, an
					incident report gets updated, an ADR moves from proposed to accepted. Static
					generation would require a full rebuild for every one of those changes, which
					defeats the point of having a CMS with a live admin panel. Server rendering
					means every request reflects the database at that exact moment, with zero
					rebuild step — the same tradeoff EmDash's own starter templates make by
					default.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="why">
				<p class="why-label">Why React islands, not a React SPA</p>
				<p>
					Out of roughly a dozen routes, exactly two need client-side state: the header's
					live search dropdown (an island EmDash itself provides) and the{" "}
					<code>/ask</code> assistant's question/answer flow (<code>AskAssistant.tsx</code>
					, mounted with <code>client:load</code>). Every other page — nav, cards, badges,
					detail pages, the knowledge listing, the search results page — is plain Astro
					and ships no framework JavaScript at all. A full SPA would mean re-implementing
					routing and SEO handling Astro already provides for free, and would ship a
					React runtime to visitors who only ever read a static article.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section id="content" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">3. Content Architecture</h2>
			<p>
				Collections aren't TypeScript interfaces hand-written into the codebase — they're
				rows in EmDash's own <code>_emdash_collections</code> / <code>_emdash_fields</code>{" "}
				tables, declared once in <code>seed/seed.json</code>, which EmDash's schema
				registry turns into real SQL tables (<code>ec_articles</code>,{" "}
				<code>ec_adrs</code>, <code>ec_incidents</code>) with typed columns — not a
				generic key-value blob. TypeScript types (<code>emdash-env.d.ts</code>) are{" "}
				<em>generated</em> from that live schema by EmDash's own tooling, so the frontend's
				types can never silently drift from the database.
			</p>
			<div class="why">
				<p class="why-label">Why EmDash as CMS infrastructure, and why structured content</p>
				<p>
					Building schema management, drafts/revisions, taxonomies, and an admin UI from
					scratch would have consumed most of the available time on infrastructure that
					already exists in this repository. Typed fields (an incident's{" "}
					<code>severity</code> enum, an ADR's <code>decision_status</code> enum) are
					what let the UI render real status badges and filters, and let the AI
					assistant extract clean plain text per document instead of scraping meaning
					out of unstructured blobs.
				</p>
			</div>
			<p>
				Tags and categories use EmDash's built-in taxonomy system (<code>getTermsForEntries</code>
				, <code>getTaxonomyTerms</code>) rather than a custom tagging table.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="api" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">4. API Architecture</h2>
			<p>There are two API surfaces, and only one of them was written for this project:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>EmDash's own REST API</strong> (<code>/_emdash/api/*</code>) — content
					CRUD, search, taxonomies, auth. Provided entirely by the CMS; EngiVault never
					reimplements this.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>/api/ask</code></strong> — the one custom endpoint, a plain
					Astro API route following Astro's own convention (a <code>POST</code> export in{" "}
					<code>src/pages/api/ask.ts</code>), delegating to a server-only module (
					<code>src/lib/knowledge-assistant.ts</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Every data access in either surface goes through EmDash's own query functions (
				<code>getEmDashCollection</code>, <code>getEmDashEntry</code>, <code>search</code>,{" "}
				<code>getTermsForEntries</code>) — no separate ORM, no raw SQL written for this
				project.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="ai" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">5. AI Architecture</h2>
			<p>
				The assistant is retrieval-augmented, not a raw pass-through to a chat model: a
				question is first matched against EmDash's content via full-text search, and only
				the matched documents' text is ever placed in the prompt.
			</p>
			<ol class="ai-steps">
				<li>Extract keywords from the question (stopwords stripped, OR-joined).</li>
				<li>
					Run EmDash's existing FTS5 <code>search()</code> across articles, ADRs, and
					incidents — top 5 matches, ranked by BM25.
				</li>
				<li>
					Fetch each match's full content and flatten it to plain text with EmDash's{" "}
					<code>extractPlainText</code>, truncated to a bounded excerpt per document.
				</li>
				<li>Send the question plus that bounded context (never the whole database) to the LLM.</li>
				<li>
					Return the model's answer alongside <code>sources</code> built from the actual
					retrieved documents — never invented by the model.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<div class="why">
				<p class="why-label">Why server-side AI calls</p>
				<p>
					The LLM provider needs a secret API key. If the browser ever held it or called
					the provider directly, it would be visible in the network tab to anyone who
					opened devtools. The React island only ever talks to this site's own{" "}
					<code>/api/ask</code>; that server-only route reads the key from{" "}
					<code>process.env</code> (never <code>import.meta.env</code>, which Vite would
					inline into the client bundle) and is the only code that ever calls the LLM.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="why">
				<p class="why-label">Why existing search instead of an external search engine</p>
				<p>
					EmDash already ships SQLite FTS5 with triggers that keep the index in sync on
					every content write — full-text search was already "free," reliable, and
					zero-ops before this project touched anything. At twelve documents, keyword
					ranking answers every example question correctly. Standing up Elasticsearch,
					Pinecone, or a vector database for that would be infrastructure looking for a
					problem — see <a href="#future">Future Evolution</a> for when that calculus
					actually changes.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section id="security" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">6. Security</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Secrets never reach the browser.</strong> The LLM key is read via{" "}
					<code>process.env</code> inside <code>/api/ask</code> and{" "}
					<code>knowledge-assistant.ts</code> only — never in a component that ships to
					the client, never hardcoded.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Input is validated before use.</strong> <code>/api/ask</code> rejects
					empty questions, questions over 500 characters, and malformed JSON with a 400
					before any retrieval or LLM call happens.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Requests are rate-limited.</strong> A per-IP, in-memory sliding window
					(10 requests/minute) returns 429 once exceeded — see the{" "}
					<a href="#scalability">Scalability</a> section for its known limit.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Upstream errors are sanitized.</strong> The LLM provider's raw error
					body is logged server-side only; the client only ever sees a generic,
					non-identifying message.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No new injection surface.</strong> All content queries go through
					EmDash's parameterized query functions; this project introduces no raw SQL.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section id="performance" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">7. Performance</h2>
			<p>
				List pages batch their per-item lookups instead of querying once per row — e.g.
				the knowledge listing fetches tags for every visible item with a single{" "}
				<code>getTermsForEntries</code> call per collection, not one query per card. The
				AI assistant bounds cost and latency the same way: five documents, each excerpted
				to roughly 900 characters, regardless of how large the knowledge base grows —
				token usage stays predictable as content scales.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="scalability" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">8. Scalability</h2>
			<p>
				Content and query load scale exactly the way any EmDash site does — the same
				SQLite/D1/Postgres story, since nothing here introduces a parallel data layer. Two
				things are honestly demo-scale today and worth naming rather than glossing over:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					The <code>/api/ask</code> rate limiter is in-memory, scoped to one process — a
					multi-instance deployment would need a shared store (Redis, or a
					database-backed counter) for it to remain correct across instances.
				</li>
				<li>
					FTS5 keyword search scales with content volume up to a point; see{" "}
					<a href="#future">Future Evolution</a> for where that point likely is.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section id="testing" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">9. Testing</h2>
			<p>
				What was actually done for this build: <code>astro check</code> (TypeScript) after
				every change, <code>astro build</code> verified after every change, and live
				browser verification of every user-facing flow — including deliberately exercising
				edge cases (empty questions, over-length questions, malformed JSON, the rate-limit
				boundary, and a zero-results search query) rather than only the happy path.
			</p>
			<p>
				No automated test suite (unit/integration/e2e) was written for this demo phase —
				worth saying plainly rather than implying otherwise. A production hardening pass
				would add vitest coverage for <code>knowledge-assistant.ts</code>'s retrieval and
				prompt-building logic against a real seeded test database, following this
				repository's own testing conventions (no DB mocks, dialect-agnostic test helpers).
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="future" class="section">
			<h2 class="section-title">10. Future Evolution</h2>
			<p>
				The current design deliberately stops short of a few things that a much larger
				knowledge base would eventually need. None of the following exists today, and
				none of it should — this is what "the next step" looks like, not a roadmap
				commitment:
			</p>
			<FlowDiagram
				tone="neutral"
				steps={[
					"CMS",
					"Event Queue",
					"Search Index",
					"Embedding Pipeline",
					"Vector Database",
					"AI Retrieval Service",
				]}
			/>
			<ul class="future-list">
				<li><strong>Event Queue</strong> — react to content publish/update events asynchronously instead of indexing inline on write.</li>
				<li><strong>Search Index</strong> — a dedicated search service, once keyword ranking on a growing corpus stops being precise enough.</li>
				<li><strong>Embedding Pipeline</strong> — generate vector embeddings for content on publish, for semantic (not just keyword) retrieval.</li>
				<li><strong>Vector Database</strong> — store and query those embeddings at a scale FTS5 wasn't designed for.</li>
				<li><strong>AI Retrieval Service</strong> — a dedicated retrieval layer the assistant calls instead of querying the CMS directly.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The trigger for actually building this isn't a fixed document count — it's when
				keyword search starts missing questions that are semantically related but share
				no literal words with the source text (a real, current limitation: EmDash's{" "}
				<code>search()</code> AND-joins bare terms, which is why <code>/api/ask</code>{" "}
				strips stopwords and OR-joins keywords before searching). Introducing a vector
				database before that point would be exactly the kind of premature complexity this
				project's own <a href="/architecture/decisions">architecture decisions</a> argue
				against.
			</p>
		</section>

		<div class="cta">
			<Button href="/architecture/decisions">View Architecture Decision Records →</Button>
		</div>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.page {
		padding-block: var(--spacing-12) var(--spacing-24);
	}

	.toc {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--spacing-2) var(--spacing-4);
		padding: var(--spacing-4) var(--spacing-5);
		margin-bottom: var(--spacing-12);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
		border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
		background: var(--color-bg-subtle);
		font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
	}

	.toc a {
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
	}

	.toc a:hover {
		color: var(--color-brand);
	}

	.section {
		max-width: var(--content-width);
		margin-bottom: var(--spacing-16);
		font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
		line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
	}

	.section p {
		margin-bottom: 1.25em;
	}

	.section ul,
	.section ol {
		margin: 0 0 1.25em;
		padding-left: 1.4em;
	}

	.section li {
		margin-bottom: 0.6em;
	}

	.section-title {
		font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
		margin-bottom: var(--spacing-5);
		padding-bottom: var(--spacing-3);
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
	}

	.flow-title {
		font-size: var(--font-size-base);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
		color: var(--color-muted);
		margin-top: var(--spacing-8);
		margin-bottom: var(--spacing-2);
	}

	.flow-caption {
		font-size: var(--font-size-base);
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
	}

	code {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.9em;
		background: var(--color-surface);
		padding: 0.15em 0.4em;
		border-radius: var(--radius);
	}

	.why {
		margin: var(--spacing-6) 0;
		padding: var(--spacing-5) var(--spacing-6);
		border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-brand) 25%, var(--color-border));
		border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
		background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-brand) 5%, var(--color-bg));
	}

	.why p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.why-label {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
		font-weight: 600;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wider);
		color: var(--color-brand);
		margin-bottom: var(--spacing-2);
	}

	.ai-steps {
		padding-left: 1.6em;
	}

	.future-list {
		font-size: var(--font-size-base);
	}

	.cta {
		display: flex;
		justify-content: center;
		padding-top: var(--spacing-8);
		border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
	}
</style>
